{{also|Appendix:Variations of “f”}}
==English==

===Etymology===
* {{sense|and the following}} From {{etyl|la}} {{term|folio||on the (next) page|lang=la}}, ablative of {{term|folium||leaf, page|lang=la}}.

==={{abbreviation}}===
'''f.'''

# and the following (page, paragraph, etc.)
# {{grammar}} [[feminine]]

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|and the following}} The abbreviation {{term||f.}} is used in a citation to refer to a section for which no final number can usefully be given. When used, {{term||f.}} has no space between it and the preceding number and is followed by a period.  If more than one following section is meant, {{term|ff.}} is used.

For example, “Hornblower 258f.” would refer to pages 258–259 while “Hornblower 258ff.” would refer to an undetermined number of pages following page 258; more casually “258–” (no end specified).

====Synonyms====
* [[et seq.]]
* [[ff.]]

